THE NULL GATE STILL FAILS, and for a DIFFERENT reason than last time -- worth
stating precisely rather than repeating "the nulls failed".

    candidate_A/A            median 0.949420  ci95 [0.906662, 1.025266]
    control_A/A              median 0.938835  ci95 [0.890318, 1.023263]
    candidate_live_pair_A/A  median 0.994113  ci95 [0.981449, 1.129099]
    live_A/A                 median 1.021569  ci95 [0.993653, 1.056437]

Last time the failure was the candidate_live_pair null at 0.911 on a host at
loadavg 38 -- i.e. the host. This time the two arms that miss the 2% band are the
candidate's and the control's OWN self-nulls (first half over second half within
one arm) at 0.949 and 0.939, while the live SciPy null is 1.022 and the
candidate/live pairing is 0.994. A host effect would move all four; it moved the
two that re-factorize.

That is the same effect `--convection-split` quantified directly: the first
sixteen-solve pass after a fresh factorization is systematically slower than an
immediately repeated one, worth ~1.6x on a solve pass. Each measurement round
here re-factorizes, so the early rounds of each arm carry more cold-solve time
than the late ones and the self-null lands below unity. It is a property of the
workload being measured, not of the measurement.

SO: the ORDER is unambiguous -- 0.63 against a widest-null threshold of 1.258,
in the loss direction, corroborated across two host states. The MAGNITUDE still
carries a caveat, and the honest way to remove it is to interleave the
factorizations across the arms rather than to keep re-running at lower load;
lowering the load did not fix it, which is itself the evidence that load was not
the cause.

BINARY FRESHNESS -- BOTH ARMS OBSERVED, on the marker this session added.
`perf_spsolve_source_marker=perf-spsolve-freshness-a` is printed in the row above.
